# Secrets and per-environment configuration for the compose stack. Copy to `.env` ON THE VM and
# fill it in. **NEVER COMMITTED, NEVER RSYNCED** — `deploy.sh` excludes it explicitly, because a
# sync with `--delete` would otherwise destroy it on every deploy.
#
# Generate the two auth secrets with:  openssl rand -base64 48

# ── The public address ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Must match the A record pointing at this VM. Caddy uses it for the certificate (FR-479) and the
# API uses it as WEB_ORIGIN.
APP_DOMAIN=
# Where Let's Encrypt sends expiry warnings. A real, monitored mailbox.
ACME_EMAIL=

# ── The database ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `postgres` is the compose service name on this VM's own private network — see
# deploy/vm/envs/*.env for why DATABASE_HOST is compared rather than assumed (FR-485).
DATABASE_HOST=postgres
# **Must differ between UAT and production.** `_common.sh` refuses to run any UAT script when
# these collide, because 001's FR-067 requires the tooling to refuse rather than merely to be
# configured correctly.
DATABASE_NAME=
POSTGRES_USER=mynet
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# ── Authentication ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# **Different values per environment, and never reused.** The pepper is mixed into every stored
# password hash: sharing it between UAT and production would mean a UAT database disclosure was a
# production one.
AUTH_PASSWORD_PEPPER=
AUTH_ATTEMPT_HASH_KEY=
AUTH_SESSION_IDLE_DAYS=14

# ── Transactional account mail ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Register entry 18 is open: no provider has been chosen. Left blank, the API keeps its
# development sink — verification and reset links are written to a log and never sent. That is
# usable for UAT and is NOT acceptable for production, where an attendee who cannot verify an
# address never becomes discoverable at all (FR-325).
MAIL_FROM=
MAIL_SMTP_URL=
# 007 — where a conduct report is sent (FR-547). Register entry 21 is open: no address has been
# named. Left blank, a report is still **stored and still blocks the reported attendee** — only
# the mail is skipped, and the API says so on startup. That fallback is deliberate: a report that
# failed because nobody had configured an inbox would be a safety control that silently did
# nothing.
MAIL_OPERATOR_ADDRESS=

# ── Web Push (007, FR-550-FR-558) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Register entry 20 is open: no push provider has been chosen and nobody holds these keys. Left
# blank, the API keeps its development sink — a notification is recorded and never delivered —
# and the client never asks for permission at all, because `isSupported()` is false without a
# public key. **Messages, unread state and the open thread are unaffected**: delivery is deniable
# by design (FR-552), so an unconfigured deployment is a complete product minus notifications.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# **BOTH HALVES OR NEITHER, AND THEY MUST BE ONE PAIR.** `config.ts` refuses to start with only
# one of them. It cannot check that they *match* — so if you generate a new pair, replace both
# lines together. A public key from one pair with a private key from another produces a push
# service that rejects every delivery with a 403 and a product that looks like it is working.
#
# Generate with:  npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
#
# The **public** half is not a secret and is compiled into the client bundle by `deploy.sh`,
# which reads it back out of this file so there is only ever one copy of it. The **private** half
# is a secret on the level of the session pepper: whoever holds it can send a notification to
# every device this deployment has ever registered.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
PUSH_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
PUSH_VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# How a push service reaches this deployment's operator, per the VAPID specification. A real
# address here is what turns a silent block by a push provider into an email.
PUSH_VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:no-reply@mynet.invalid

# ── Administration (011, FR-919a, FR-919b, FR-991) ────────────────────────────────────────────
# The second website, served by Caddy at `admin.{$APP_DOMAIN}` (constitution v4.1.0, decision 37).
# Its A record has to resolve independently of the apex one, or Caddy cannot obtain the second
# certificate — see deploy/vm/README.md.
#
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# An administrative session is bounded TWICE, and the two bounds answer different questions.
#
# The idle window is advanced on every use, so it asks "has this person stopped working?" — that
# is the same shape as the attendee session's AUTH_SESSION_IDLE_DAYS, only far shorter, because
# an unattended administrative browser is a different exposure from an unattended attendee one.
#
# The absolute cap is **fixed when the session is established and never advanced**, so it asks
# "how long may one sign-in last, no matter how busy?" A session that is used every four minutes
# would never expire under an idle rule alone. `config.ts` stores the resolved instant on the
# session row rather than deriving it, so **changing these values does not retroactively extend
# or truncate a session that is already open** — the value that applied when it opened governs it.
#
# Left blank, both fall back to the defaults below. Neither can be disabled: there is no value
# meaning "no expiry", and a zero or negative one fails at boot rather than starting.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# **LEAVE `ADMIN_ORIGIN` UNSET IN EVERY DEPLOYED ENVIRONMENT. IT IS A DEVELOPMENT-ONLY SETTING.**
#
# Caddy serves the administrative site at `admin.{$APP_DOMAIN}` and reverse-proxies `/api/*`
# beneath it, so administrative requests are **same-origin** and never reach CORS — which is the
# whole reason decision 37 chose a subdomain: it keeps `connect-src 'self'` literally true and the
# host-only session cookie sendable.
#
# Locally there is no proxy and no DNS, so the two clients are two ports — two origins — and a
# credentialed request from the administrative one is refused with an opaque network failure.
# Setting this adds that origin to the API's CORS allow-list, and setting it *here* would widen
# the allow-list in production to an origin nobody named.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
ADMIN_ORIGIN=

ADMIN_SESSION_IDLE_MINUTES=30
ADMIN_SESSION_ABSOLUTE_HOURS=8

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# **THE SEED CREATES PLATFORM-OPERATOR IDENTITIES WITH NO CREDENTIAL, AND THESE TWO ARE HOW ONE
# GETS A CREDENTIAL FOR THE FIRST TIME** (FR-990, FR-991, FR-992).
#
# The seed is committed, reviewed, world-readable data — this repository is public — so it cannot
# carry an administrative password the way it carries throwaway attendee ones. It seeds the
# identity with a null `password_hash`, which means **sign-in is impossible rather than
# defaulted**: there is no well-known password to find and no account to guess your way into.
#
# Left blank, that is exactly where it stays, and nobody can sign in to the administrative site
# at all. That is a safe state, not a broken one, and it is the correct state for any environment
# where no human has yet been made responsible for it (register entry 21 is still open).
#
# `pnpm admin:bootstrap` reads these two, sets the credential on the matching seeded identity,
# and marks it initial — so the first sign-in is forced to replace it before reaching any
# administrative surface. Running it again NEVER resets an operator who has already replaced
# their credential (FR-993); recovery from a lost one is a re-seed, deliberately.
#
# **This is separate from `pnpm db:seed` on purpose.** Seeding deletes every attendee and
# re-inserts the committed fixtures; bootstrapping a credential must not require doing that.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_EMAIL=
ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD=

# ── Avatars ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both renditions come from one decode through `images/avatar.ts` (FR-458). Raising the card
# dimension raises the size of every directory response, since the card rendition is embedded.
AVATAR_DIMENSION_PX=512
AVATAR_CARD_DIMENSION_PX=96

# ── Backups (FR-487) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where `backup.sh` writes dumps on this machine, and how many it keeps.
BACKUP_DIR=/home/azureuser/app/deploy/vm/backups
BACKUP_KEEP_LOCAL=7
# Refuse to run when free space is below this, rather than filling the disk the live database
# writes to. A backup that stops PostgreSQL from writing is worse than a missing backup.
BACKUP_MIN_FREE_MB=2048
